      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=r^{2}$, find $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}=\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202r}=2 r,\\,\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =(2 r)\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =2 r \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
