      ":question/id": "0480635c-deef-558b-bf50-7e4a5cabfaac",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=- r^{2} (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2)$, find $\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}=\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202r}=- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2),\\,\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202\\theta}=4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =(- 2 r (\\cos {4 \\theta} - 2))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(4 r^{2} \\sin {4 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =4r\\cos \\theta-2r\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta-4r\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-r(-4\\cos \\theta+2\\cos 4\\theta\\cos \\theta+4\\sin 4\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
