      ":question/problem": "Given that $u(r,\\theta)=- 3 r^{3} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3)$, find $\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202x}=\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202r}=- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3),\\,\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202\\theta}=- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202x} & =(- 9 r^{2} (\\sin {2 \\theta} - 3))\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(- 6 r^{3} \\cos {2 \\theta})\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =27{r}^{2}\\cos \\theta-9{r}^{2}\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta+6{r}^{2}\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =-3{r}^{2}(-9\\cos \\theta+3\\sin 2\\theta\\cos \\theta-2\\cos 2\\theta\\sin \\theta).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
